WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.133
The eval set comes before the system.

2
00:00:02.413 --> 00:00:07.896
The most important ordering rule in the capstone: build the eval set before you build the system.

3
00:00:08.176 --> 00:00:09.392
There are two reasons.

4
00:00:09.672 --> 00:00:10.952
The first is bias.

5
00:00:11.232 --> 00:00:18.144
Once a system exists, you unconsciously write test cases it can handle, because those are the cases you have been looking at.

6
00:00:18.424 --> 00:00:23.565
Cases written first describe the user’s job; cases written later describe your system.

7
00:00:23.845 --> 00:00:34.512
Anthropic’s guide to agent evals makes the same practical point from the other side: evals get harder to build the longer you wait, and you don’t need hundreds of tasks to start.

8
00:00:34.792 --> 00:00:44.840
It suggests that 20 to 50 tasks drawn from real failures is a good beginning, and that the manual checks you already run during development are a natural source.

9
00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:48.683
The second reason is that the eval set is the specification.

10
00:00:48.963 --> 00:01:00.803
Writing 100 cases with expected outcomes forces decisions you would otherwise defer: what counts as a correct answer, when the system should abstain, which mistakes are worse than others.

11
00:01:01.083 --> 00:01:06.288
For the capstone, aim for at least 100 cases in week 1 and grow it through week 4.

12
00:01:06.568 --> 00:01:21.352
Tag each by source: realistic cases written from the user’s job, synthetic cases generated for coverage (and spot-checked), should-abstain cases the system must decline, and adversarial cases such as injected instructions.

13
00:01:21.632 --> 00:01:36.459
Split it into dev and a sealed held-out set of at least a quarter of the cases, as Hamel Husain’s evals FAQ and Studio 3 describe, and decide now how rarely you will run held-out (end of weeks 2, 4, and 6 is a good rule).

14
00:01:36.739 --> 00:01:45.016
Prefer binary pass or fail labels with a written reason, because they are faster to label consistently and easier to put an interval on.
